title: The clock channel
story: fernhill
description: The case clock is the story's only addressable channel — a
  sidebar-gated `clock` channel fed by `emit estate-clock`. This is the
  `[CHANNEL:]` assertion family, which no document mentions.
seed: 1
channels: clock

---

> north
[OK: contains "Gravel Drive"]

> north
[OK: contains "Fountain Court"]

> north
[OK: contains "Entrance Hall"]
[OK: contains "a tall case clock standing silent against the stairs"]

# The clock is stopped, so nothing has ever been emitted on `estate-clock` and
# the channel has never carried a value. Sparse-channel silence is assertable.
[CHANNEL: clock, is absent]

# `wearable, a container` — the coat is searchable and the key is `concealed`,
# the same pattern as the doormat in key.transcript.
> examine the grey overcoat
[OK: contains "The pockets are deep"]

> search the grey overcoat
[OK: contains "winding key"]

> take the winding key
[OK: contains "You take the winding key from the grey overcoat"]

# `define trait windable` — the player must hold the key, and the clock must be
# `stopped`.
> wind the case clock
[OK: contains "the old clock clears its throat and begins to walk again: tick, tock"]

# `it must be stopped: clock-already-going` — the second winding is refused,
# and it still costs a turn, which the count below depends on.
> wind the case clock
[OK: contains "It is wound and walking. Tick, tock"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

# Turn 14. Two things land together: the long-night sequence flips the story to
# `midnight`, and the clock's `on every turn while it is ticking and one chance
# in 8` rolls a chime. The chime's `emit estate-clock with hour "past midnight"
# when midnight` sees the state AFTER the flip.
#
# This is only reliable because the seed is pinned — at one chance in 8, an
# unpinned run puts the chime somewhere else entirely, or nowhere in this file.
> wait
[OK: contains "Midnight is coming on"]
[OK: contains "The case clock counts the quarter, bright and even, through the whole house"]

# FINDING, asserted as it behaves: the channel says NOTHING, on this turn or
# any other. The chime fired — its prose is in the output above — and
# `emit estate-clock with hour "past midnight" when midnight` sits directly
# under that chime in the story, but `return "The clock: (hour)" from
# estate-clock` never produces a value in a transcript run.
#
# Checked by probe rather than inferred: a 46-turn run with the clock ticking
# throughout, asserting `[CHANNEL: clock, is absent]` on every single turn,
# passes on every single turn. The channel is silent for the whole story.
#
# The channel is declared `gated by sidebar`, and a transcript session has no
# sidebar. `channels: clock` in this header declares which channels the run
# CAPTURES; it does not open a gate. So a gated channel appears to be
# unassertable from a transcript at all — which means the sidebar clock, one of
# the four things this story exists to demonstrate, has no test coverage
# available to it.
#
# The failure message when this was written as `is present` does not mention
# gating. It says the channel "said nothing this turn — if that is the claim,
# write [CHANNEL: clock, is absent]", which reads as a normal sparse turn and
# sends the author to write exactly the assertion below, concluding their story
# works.
[CHANNEL: clock, is absent]
